# Reading a `.tree` file.
#
# THROUGH THE COMPILER, never by hand. Every custom `.tree` dialect is parsed
# by the Term compiler into a CST and an AST, and integrated by defining a
# mill. Zone's grammar is at `@term/mill/code/zone`, beside every other
# dialect's, and this file is the one call that reaches it.
#
# WHAT WAS HERE BEFORE, and why it is gone. 286 lines that split on spaces,
# tracked indent depth by hand and kept ONE `<...>` per line. It read as "a
# strict subset of Term syntax", which is the excuse that makes a second
# implementation of a grammar sound safe. It was not: a line carrying two
# values silently lost the first, so `time <made>, <toss>` parsed as a cache
# with no fetch time and read as stale the instant it was written. The file
# format was then bent around a limitation the language never had.
#
# A second implementation of a grammar disagrees with the grammar eventually.
# There is one parser now, and it is the compiler's.
#
# The shape produced is what `@term/deck/code/read.ts` flattens the compiler's
# parse into:
#
#   head   the first word
#   term   the bare words after it, in order
#   code   the FIRST `<...>` value, empty when the line carries none
#   more   EVERY `<...>` value, in order, so a multi-value line keeps them all
#   leaf   nested lines, from an indent or from a comma, in written order

load @term/seed/code/text
  find text

load @term/seed/code/list
  find list

load ../base
  find leaf

# The shim that carries the compiler's parser. `native/node/runtime/mill.ts`.
dock load
  load <global:mill>, name mill

# The compiler's reader, reached as a native call.
#
# It is bundled self-contained by `pnpm run make:read` in the deck package,
# so it carries its own parser and needs nothing resolvable beside it.
bind do-mine-tree
  take body, like text
  like list
  case node
    text <mill.read($body)>

# Parse a `.tree` file into leaves. A native `bind` is file-local, so this
# wrapper is what the rest of zone imports.
task mine-tree
  take body, like text
  like list
  send back
    call do-mine-tree
      read body

# The reader for a stored secret's NOTE, which is a person's field and
# may be prose or carry `\r\n` endings. The shim normalises the endings
# and answers an empty list where the parse fails, where `mine-tree`
# stops the run: a declaration we own that does not parse is a bug, a
# note somebody typed is not.
bind do-mine-note
  take body, like text
  like list
  case node
    text <mill.note($body)>

# Parse a note into leaves, or none.
task mine-note
  take body, like text
  like list
  send back
    call do-mine-note
      read body
